 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The worker manages the 12 GiB cache with a byte-bounded 2Q policy composed of two resident queues and one metadata-only ghost queue:
+
+- **`A1in` (FIFO, resident probation queue)**: Receives newly rendered sheets on first admission. Target byte share is 25% of the cache budget (3 GiB by default, configurable).
+- **`A1out` (FIFO, metadata-only ghost queue)**: Records cache keys `(clip-id, revision, renderer-version)` evicted from `A1in` after their JPEG files are deleted. Bounded by entry count (default 3,000 keys, roughly half the ~6,000-sheet capacity at ~2 MiB per sheet).
+- **`Am` (LRU, resident main queue)**: Holds sheets that have demonstrated reuse, occupying the remaining 75% of the cache budget (9 GiB by default).
+
+**Cache hits:**
+- On a hit in `Am`, serve the JPEG and move the entry to the most-recently-used end of `Am`.
+- On a hit in `A1in`, serve the JPEG without promoting or reordering the entry, preventing short-burst sequential scans from promoting one-use sheets into `Am`.
+
+**Eviction and admission:**
+- Before admitting a completed sheet of size `S` bytes, evict resident entries until `resident_bytes + S <= 12 GiB`. For each eviction step: if `A1in` is at or above its target byte share (or `Am` is empty), evict the oldest entry from `A1in`, delete its JPEG file, and append its key to `A1out` (dropping the oldest key from `A1out` if at its entry limit); otherwise, evict the least-recently-used entry from `Am` and delete its JPEG file without recording it in `A1out`.
+- Admit the completed sheet: if its key is present in `A1out`, remove the key from `A1out` and insert the entry at the most-recently-used end of `Am`; otherwise, insert it at the tail of `A1in`.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (`A1in` FIFO probation, `A1out` ghost FIFO, `Am` LRU main) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparison of a small working set. Plain LRU minimizes bookkeeping and adapts quickly on project switches, but sequential screenings evict active comparison sheets. Frequency-based eviction with aging retains popular clips, but adds counter and aging overhead and can retain stale favorites across project switches. 2Q isolates one-use screening sheets in `A1in` so reused comparison sheets stay protected in `Am`, accepting extra queue bookkeeping and a tunable size split while keeping all replacement state private to the preview worker. |
+
